  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "The area of a square doubles when its side length is increased by $2$. Find the side length of the original square.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$2+2\\sqrt2$ units.",
      "worked": "Let the original side length be $s>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(s+2)^2&=2s^2\\\\s^2+4s+4&=2s^2\\\\s^2-4s-4&=0\\\\s&=\\frac{4\\pm\\sqrt{(-4)^2-4(1)(-4)}}{2(1)}\\\\s&=\\frac{4\\pm\\sqrt{32}}2\\\\s&=2\\pm2\\sqrt2\\end{align*}$$\nReject $2-2\\sqrt2<0$. The original side length is $2+2\\sqrt2$ units.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 70
  },
